The earliest project here, from my first engineering role.

## The platform

A public developer API for one of the largest telecom providers in the Philippines.
Other businesses built against it to put carrier-level capabilities into their own
products: SMS, voice, location, rewards, geofencing and USSD.

## A public API changes the rules

An internal API can be fixed by messaging the one team that calls it. A public one
cannot. Someone else's production depends on the response shape you already shipped,
so changes have to be additive, versions have to be real, and removing anything needs a
deprecation path rather than a rename and an apology. Learning that constraint early,
on a platform where the callers were strangers, was the most useful thing I took from
the job.

## Running it

I owned a good share of the operations work: keeping services up and monitored,
handling deploys and scaling, and writing the root-cause analysis after an incident.
Writing RCAs is what trained me to look past the thing that broke and ask why the
system allowed it to break. Most of that was AWS day to day.

## Documentation as part of the product

I wrote the API documentation and the onboarding guides external developers used. On a
public API the docs are not a chore attached to the work, they are the surface most
integrators actually touch. An endpoint nobody can work out how to call may as well not
exist. Writing them also turned out to be the fastest way to find design flaws, because
having to explain an awkward interface in plain language makes the awkwardness
impossible to ignore.

## Stack

Flask and Node.js in a microservices setup on AWS, using EC2, API Gateway, Lambda and
CloudWatch.
